#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059528 :question/problem "Let $A(1,3)$ and $B(2,-2)$. Find $\\mid \\overrightarrow{AB} \\mid$.\n\n![](images/f9/f95ee3c5c81750d97019d910350e4c78f0962baa682c834928450a451691145a.png)" :question/worked-solution "We have\n\n$$\\overset{\\to}{OA} = a = i + 3j,; \\overset{\\to}{OB} = b = 2i - 2j$$\n\nExpressing $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\overset{\\to}{AB} &= \\overset{\\to}{OB} - \\overset{\\to}{OA} \\\\\n&= b - a \\\\\n&= (2i - 2j) - (i + 3j) \\\\\n&= i - 5j\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\overset{\\to}{AB} \\mid &= \\sqrt{1^{2} + (-5)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1 + 25} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{26}\n\\end{aligned}$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059530 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 2 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}$ and $\\mathbf{b}={\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]}.$ Find a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}$." :question/worked-solution "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{u} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}}{|\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}|}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFirst, let's compute the sum:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2+2 \\\\ 0+(-2)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid a + 2b \\mid &= \\sqrt{0^{2} + (-2)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{u} & =\\frac{\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}}{|\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059543 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 0,-0.4,11 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 2,5,2 \\rangle.$ Find $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "Summing up the products of corresponding components, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b} & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =0\\cdot 2+(-0.4)\\cdot 5+11\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =0-2+22 \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059544 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}4 \\\\ - 2 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}$ and $\\mathbf{b} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ t\\end{array} \\right]}$. Given that $\\mathbf{a} \\perp \\mathbf{b}$, find the value of $t$." :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\mathbf{a} \\perp \\mathbf{b}$ ($\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are perpendicular) if and only if $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{b} = 0.$\n\nComputing the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b} & ={a}_{x}\\cdot {b}_{x}+{a}_{y}\\cdot {b}_{y}+{a}_{z}\\cdot {b}_{z} \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-2)+(-2)\\cdot 5+1\\cdot t \\\\  & =-8-10+t \\\\  & =t-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b} & =0 \\\\ t-18 & =0 \\\\ t & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059545 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 3,-4,1 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle -7,0,2 \\rangle.$ Find $\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & -4 & 1 \\\\ -7 & 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -7 & 2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ -7 & 0\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-4)\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 0)i-(3\\cdot 2-1\\cdot (-7))j+(3\\cdot 0-(-4)\\cdot (-7))k \\\\  & =-8i-13j-28k \\\\  & =⟨-8,-13,-28⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059546 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 3, 1, 2x \\rangle,$ $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 0,1,x \\rangle,$ and $|\\,\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{b}\\,| = \\sqrt{19}.$ Find $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & 1 & 2x \\\\ 0 & 1 & x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2x \\\\ 1 & x\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2x \\\\ 0 & x\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =-xi-3xj+3k \\\\  & =⟨-x,-3x,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,a\\times b\\,| & =\\sqrt{(-x)^{2}+(-3x)^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10{x}^{2}+9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & |\\,a\\times b\\,|=\\sqrt{19} \\\\  & \\sqrt{10{x}^{2}+9}=\\sqrt{19} \\\\  & 10{x}^{2}+9=19 \\\\  & 10{x}^{2}=10 \\\\  & {x}^{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x= \\pm 1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059557 :question/problem "Calculate $\\det(A),$ if <div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "For a $2 \\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix} = ad - bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 6-2\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =18-10 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059558 :question/problem "Calculate $\\det(A),$ if <div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & -2 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "For a $2 \\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix} = ad - bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & -2 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}-(-2)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059559 :question/problem "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle {A} = \n    \\left\\lbrack\n    \\begin{matrix}\n        2 & -1  \\\\\n        -3 & k\n    \\end{matrix}\n    \\right\\rbrack\n$$\nand\n$\\det({A}) = 5$, what is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "For a $2 \\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix} = ad - bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & k\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot k-(-1)\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =2k-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\det(A)=5,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =5 \\\\ 2k-3 & =5 \\\\ 2k & =8 \\\\ k & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059958 :question/problem "The inequality $-10\\le x\\le 3$ can be expressed in interval notation as" :question/worked-solution "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses $()$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets $[]$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nOur inequality contains only \"or equal to\" symbols. So, the endpoints $-10$ and $3$ *are* included. \n\n$$\n-10\\,\\,\\le \\,\\,x\\,\\,\\le \\,\\,3\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express our inequality in interval notation as follows:\n\n$$\n[-10,3]\n$$\n\nMore formally, we can also write\n\n$$\nx\\in [-10,3].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059959 :question/problem "The inequality $3 < x < 7$ can be expressed in interval notation as" :question/worked-solution "The strict inequalities exclude both endpoints. Use parentheses at both ends: $x\\in(3,7)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059980 :question/problem "Calculate $\\text{det}(A)$, if\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(A) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =0-6 \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059981 :question/problem "What is $\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}$?" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{2} & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\n&=\\sqrt{2}\\cdot3-5\\cdot1 \\\\\n&=3\\sqrt{2}-5.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059982 :question/problem "Calculate $det(Z)$, if\n $$\n\\displaystyle Z=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{x} & y \\\\ {y}^{2} & \\sqrt{x}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(Z) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{x} & y \\\\ {y}^{2} & \\sqrt{x}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{x}\\cdot \\sqrt{x}-y\\cdot {y}^{2} \\\\  & =x-{y}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060000 :question/problem "What is $\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{vmatrix}$?" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 7\\end{vmatrix}\n&=2\\cdot7-(-4)\\cdot(-2) \\\\\n&=14-8 \\\\\n&=6.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060001 :question/problem "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle C = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & k\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand\n$det(C) = 3$, what is the value of $k$?" :question/worked-solution "For a $2\\times 2$ matrix, the determinant is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{vmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{vmatrix}=ad-bc.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\det(C)&=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & k\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=2\\cdot k-3\\cdot5 \\\\\n&=2k-15.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\det(C)=3$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2k-15&=3 \\\\\n2k&=18 \\\\\nk&=9.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060012 :question/problem "The inequality $9 < x \\le 13$ can be expressed in interval notation as" :question/worked-solution "The lower endpoint $9$ is excluded, and the upper endpoint $13$ is included. Thus $x\\in(9,13]$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060020 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $M$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5} & 0 & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 & 3 & 5 \\\\ -3\\sqrt{5} & 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\sqrt{5} & 0 & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 & 3 & 5 \\\\ -3\\sqrt{5} & 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix}-0\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ -3\\sqrt{5} & 4\\end{vmatrix}+\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -3\\sqrt{5} & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}(12-15)-0+\\frac{1}{3}(0+9\\sqrt{5}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\cdot (-3)+\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 9\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =-3\\sqrt{5}+3\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $det(M)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statement “$M$ is a singular matrix” is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060025 :question/problem "If $\\mathbf{u}=⟨12,5⟩$ and $\\mathbf{v}=⟨9,-2⟩$, then $\\mathbf{u}-\\mathbf{v}=$" :question/worked-solution "Subtract corresponding components:\n$$\\langle12,5\\rangle-\\langle9,-2\\rangle=\\langle12-9,5-(-2)\\rangle=\\langle3,7\\rangle.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060028 :question/problem "If $\\overset{\\to }{u}=⟨4,1⟩$ and $\\overset{\\to }{v}=⟨1,-4⟩$, then $\\overset{\\to }{u}-\\overset{\\to }{v}=$" :question/worked-solution "Subtract corresponding components:\n$$\\langle4,1\\rangle-\\langle1,-4\\rangle=\\langle3,5\\rangle.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060029 :question/problem "If $\\mathbf{v}=⟨4,-1⟩$, then what is the magnitude of $\\mathbf{v}$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/worked-solution "Use the Euclidean norm: $$\\|v\\|=\\sqrt{4^2+(-1)^2}=\\sqrt{17}.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060030 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{u}=⟨-3,2⟩$ and $\\mathbf{v}=⟨7,9⟩.$ Find $\\mathbf{u}\\cdot \\mathbf{v}.$" :question/worked-solution "Summing up the products of corresponding components, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{u}\\cdot \\mathbf{v} & ={u}_{x}\\cdot {v}_{x}+{u}_{y}\\cdot {v}_{y} \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot 7+2\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =-21+18 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060031 :question/problem "If $a = ⟨ - 1, 0, 6⟩$ and $b = ⟨10, 2,-5⟩$, then $a \\cdot b =$" :question/worked-solution "Multiply corresponding components and add:\n\n$$\na\\cdot b=(-1)(10)+(0)(2)+(6)(-5)=-10-30=-40.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060041 :question/problem "If $\\overset{\\to }{v}=⟨8,-7⟩$, then $3\\overset{\\to }{v}=$" :question/worked-solution "Multiply each component by $3$:\n$$3\\langle8,-7\\rangle=\\langle24,-21\\rangle.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060046 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{u}=⟨-3,10,4⟩$ and $\\mathbf{v}=⟨4,7,1⟩.$ Find $\\mathbf{u}\\times \\mathbf{v}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{u}\\times \\mathbf{v} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -3 & 10 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 7 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}10 & 4 \\\\ 7 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 10 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(10\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 7)\\mathbf{i}-((-3)\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 4)\\mathbf{j}+((-3)\\cdot 7-10\\cdot 4)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(10-28)\\mathbf{i}-(-3-16)\\mathbf{j}+(-21-40)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-18\\mathbf{i}+19\\mathbf{j}-61\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-18,19,-61⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060047 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{u}=⟨\\frac{1}{3},9,6⟩$ and $\\mathbf{v}=⟨0,-3,3⟩$. Find $\\mathbf{u}\\times \\mathbf{v}$." :question/worked-solution "The source asks for the cross product. Expanding its determinant gives\n$$\\mathbf u\\times\\mathbf v=\\left\\langle9(3)-6(-3),\\ 6(0)-\\frac13(3),\\ \\frac13(-3)-9(0)\\right\\rangle=\\langle45,-1,-1\\rangle.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060071 :question/problem "What is $(\\sin x - \\cos x)^{2} + (\\sin x + \\cos x)^{2}$?" :question/worked-solution "Expand the two squares. Their mixed terms cancel, so $$(\\sin x-\\cos x)^2+(\\sin x+\\cos x)^2=2\\sin^2 x+2\\cos^2 x=2.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060072 :question/problem "What is $\\frac{\\cos x}{1 - \\sin x} - \\tan x$?" :question/worked-solution "On the domain of the original expression, rationalize and simplify:\n$$\\frac{\\cos x}{1-\\sin x}-\\tan x=\\frac{1+\\sin x}{\\cos x}-\\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}=\\frac1{\\cos x}=\\sec x.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060118 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨-2,1⟩$ and $\\mathbf{b}=⟨4,-1⟩$. Find $\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}$." :question/worked-solution "First scale, then add components:\n$$\\langle-2,1\\rangle+2\\langle4,-1\\rangle=\\langle-2+8,1-2\\rangle=\\langle6,-1\\rangle.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060119 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b}$ and points the same direction." :question/worked-solution "First $a+3b=\\langle-1,0\\rangle+3\\langle3,-2\\rangle=\\langle8,-6\\rangle$. Its norm is $10$. Dividing by this positive norm preserves direction and gives the unit vector $\\langle4/5,-3/5\\rangle$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060124 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$, $\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{c}=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$. Find $\\mathbf{a}-(2\\mathbf{b}-3\\mathbf{c})$." :question/worked-solution "Compute $2\\mathbf b-3\\mathbf c=\\langle6,6\\rangle-\\langle-24,12\\rangle=\\langle30,-6\\rangle$. Therefore\n$$\\mathbf a-(2\\mathbf b-3\\mathbf c)=\\langle4,-2\\rangle-\\langle30,-6\\rangle=\\langle-26,4\\rangle.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060125 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$, $\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{c}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$. Find $\\mathbf{a}-(2\\mathbf{b}+5\\mathbf{c})$." :question/worked-solution "Compute $2\\mathbf b+5\\mathbf c=\\langle8,2\\rangle+\\langle-5,10\\rangle=\\langle3,12\\rangle$. Therefore\n$$\\mathbf a-(2\\mathbf b+5\\mathbf c)=\\langle1,1\\rangle-\\langle3,12\\rangle=\\langle-2,-11\\rangle.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060126 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨-12,3⟩$. Find $|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\hspace{0.167em}|$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/worked-solution "Use the Euclidean norm: $$\\|a\\|=\\sqrt{(-12)^2+3^2}=\\sqrt{153}=3\\sqrt{17}.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060127 :question/problem "![](images/2c/2c1f9580f07d06af7ec900ee63c95872e06f459107e64cf1b5117d928efdab22.png)\n\nLet $A(-4, 1)$ and $B(3, 0)$. Find $\\mid \\overset{\\to}{AB} \\mid$." :question/worked-solution "The displacement is $\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\langle3-(-4),0-1\\rangle=\\langle7,-1\\rangle$. Its length is $$\\sqrt{7^2+(-1)^2}=\\sqrt{50}=5\\sqrt2.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060128 :question/problem "![](images/0d/0d92a4d7b9c59b0d6bc8ffec9926bcf189c9bbe808c5bed7a02d6ab09bcebcd0.png)\n\nLet $P(-2, 2)$ and $Q(3, 3)$. Find $\\mid \\overset{\\to}{PQ} \\mid$." :question/worked-solution "The displacement is $\\overrightarrow{PQ}=\\langle3-(-2),3-2\\rangle=\\langle5,1\\rangle$. Its length is $$\\sqrt{5^2+1^2}=\\sqrt{26}.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060129 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$ and points the same direction." :question/worked-solution "The norm of $a$ is $\\sqrt{3^2+8^2}=\\sqrt{73}$. Dividing by this positive number preserves direction and gives $$\\frac{a}{\\|a\\|}=\\begin{bmatrix}3/\\sqrt{73}\\\\8/\\sqrt{73}\\end{bmatrix}.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060184 :question/problem "Let $u = \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $v = \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$. Given that $u⊥v$ and $x > 0$, find the value of $x$." :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular vectors have dot product zero. Thus\n\n$$\nu\\cdot v=x^2+(-2)(2)=x^2-4=0.\n$$\n\nThe roots are $x=\\pm2$. Since $x>0$, the required value is $x=2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060185 :question/problem "What is the value of $x$ given that $a = ⟨x,-6x, 3⟩, b = ⟨x, 1, 3⟩$ and $a⊥b$?" :question/worked-solution "Since $a\\perp b$, their dot product is zero:\n\n$$\na\\cdot b=x^2-6x+9=(x-3)^2=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore $x=3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060193 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨1,2,3x⟩$, $\\mathbf{b}=⟨4,3,x⟩$ and $|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}|=\\sqrt{705}$. Find $x$.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." :question/worked-solution "The source condition is the magnitude of a cross product. Compute\n$$\\mathbf a\\times\\mathbf b=\\langle-7x,11x,-5\\rangle.$$\nSquaring the magnitude equation gives $170x^2+25=705$, so $x^2=4$ and $x=\\pm2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060194 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨2x,1,4⟩,$ $\\mathbf{b}=⟨2,0,3⟩$ and $|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}|=\\sqrt{13}.$ Find $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2x & 1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(3-0)\\mathbf{i}-(6x-8)\\mathbf{j}+(0-2)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨3,-(6x-8),-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the magnitude, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-(6x-8))^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+36{x}^{2}-96x+64+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36{x}^{2}-96x+77}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we solve for $x$ the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| & =\\sqrt{13} \\\\ \\sqrt{36{x}^{2}-96x+77} & =\\sqrt{13} \\\\ 36{x}^{2}-96x+77 & =13 \\\\ 36{x}^{2}-96x+64 & =0 \\\\ 9{x}^{2}-24x+16 & =0 \\\\ (3x-4)^{2} & =0 \\\\ 3x-4 & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=\\frac{4}{3}.$"}]}
